    "https://jobs.uri.edu/postings/16861": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Disability Access & Student Support",
      "description": "University Of Rhode Island Applicant Portal | Coordinator, Disability Access &amp; Student Support Skip to Main Content University Of Rhode Island H uman R esource A dministration 80 Lower College Road, Kingston, RI 02881 urihr@ucs.uri.edu - 401.874.2416 - 401.874.5741 (fax) URI Homepage Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help About URI Office of Human Resources Office of Equal Opportunity Campus Maps & Directions Hours: Monday - Friday 8.30am to 4.30pm Coordinator, Disability Access & Student Support Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening.  To apply for the position, please click the Apply for this Job link/button. If you would like to bookmark this position for later review, click on the Bookmark link. If you would like to print a copy of this position for your records, click on the Print Preview link. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please attach the following 2 (PDF) documents to your online Employment Application: (#1) Cover letter. (#2) Resume. Note: References will be upon request by the search committee. Posting Details Position Information Posting Number SF02179 Job Title Coordinator, Disability Access & Student Support Position Number 109328 FTE 1.00 FLSA Exempt Position Type Professional Staff Union PSA/NEA - Professional Staff Assoc Pay Grade Level Grade Level: 11 Pay Grade Range Anticipated Hiring Salary Range: $70,000 - $75,000 Status Calendar Year, Full-time, Permanent Department Information Department VP Student Affrs. Contact(s) Please note: Job applications must be submitted directly online only at: (https://jobs.uri.edu) Contact Phone/Extension Contact Email Physical Demands Campus Location Kingston Grant Funded No Extension Contingency Notes Job Description Summary The search will remain open until the position has been filled. First consideration will be given to applications received by September 14, 2026. Applications received after September 14, 2026 may be reviewed depending on search progress and needs but are not guaranteed full consideration. ________________________________________________________________________________________________________ About URI : The University of Rhode Island enrolls approximately 17,000 students across its graduate and undergraduate programs and is the State’s flagship public research university, as well as the land grant and sea grant university, for the state of Rhode Island. The main campus is located in the historic village of Kingston, and the Bay Campus is located in Narragansett. Both campuses are near major beaches in a beautiful coastal community. URI is just 30 minutes from Providence, RI and within easy reach of Newport, Boston, and New York City. ________________________________________________________________________________________________________ POSITION SUMMARY : Support students through challenges, connect them to appropriate campus and community resources, and empower/ promote agency to enhance well-being . Collaboratively coordinate and deliver university accessibility services and support for students with disabilities. Duties and Responsibilities KEY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES : Under the supervision of the Assistant Deans, implement case management and individual intervention strategies for students needing behavioral intervention (i.e. , disruptive students, substance abuse prevention, emotional well-being, and safety. ) Services will be provided to students during in—person and/or remote meetings . Establish and supervise delivery and database documentation of essential services to students with disabilities, including reasonable accommodations to support equal opportunity in URI academic and co—curricular programs and services. Serve as a liaison with Housing and Residential Life to support students with documented disability needs and/or housing insecurity. Serve in a triage/ referral capacity to provide support and assessment for the type of resources and assistance needed for academic and personal success, including appropriate disability—related options. Examples include mental health concerns, medical emergencies, family challenges, food insecurity, financial hardship, and bereavement . Assist and advise the Assistant Deans and the DAI / SSAS Teams in guiding University departments regarding students of concern along with accessibility options in courses, programs, and services; supply guidance for providing resources and services to students with disabilities and/or students of concern. Design and implement individual, group, and environmental interventions to increase awareness of mental health problems, available supports and decreases impact of disruptive student behavior on all aspects of campus life. Provide follow—up case management support for students referred to through the University Student Assistance Teams, and for students’ post—hospitalization. Work directly with students and coordinate with appropriate university personnel including University Police, Counseling Center, Health Services, Retention and Academic Success, Violence Prevention and Advocacy Services, Talent Development, Academic Deans, as well as local hospitals and police agencies OTHER DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES : Serve as a trainer in areas of disruptive student behavior and student mental health concerns for faculty and staff. Compile statistical reports and documents for the Dean of Students Office . Represent the Office on University, Division, and other committees, and at University events, such as commencement, new student orientation, open houses and welcome days. Perform other duties as required. LICENSES , TOOLS AND EQUIPMENT : Personal computers, printers, word processing, database management and spreadsheet software. Required Qualifications QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED : Master’s degree in social work, counseling, psychology or college student personnel. Minimum of two years of administrative or instructional experience working with college students in a higher education setting. Ability to manage highly sensitive and confidential communications. Crisis management experience. Experience with issues of disability. Working knowledge of Federal Regulations 504, Americans with Disabilities Act Amendment Act, and EERPA . Experience with accommodation determinations. Strong verbal and interpersonal communication skills. Proficiency in written communication skills. Understanding of student development theory. Knowledge of common issues and challenges facing a diverse population of undergraduate and graduate students. Ability to work in a fast—paced, collaborative team environment. Willingness to work nonstandard hours (e.g. , evenings, weekends, late nights). Experience with technology (i.e. Word, Excel, PowerPoint) : Decision—making skills. Ability to work with diverse groups/ populations . Preferred Qualifications PREFERRED : Experience with Student Management software ( i . e. Maxient, Starfish, or similar software). ________________________________________________________________________________________________________ EEO Statement: URI is unwavering in its commitment to equal opportunity, community, equity, and diversity. In furtherance of its mission to serve and support all learners as the state’s public flagship university, URI continues to expand efforts to recruit and retain a broadly representative workforce as well as to regularly evaluate the effectiveness of those efforts. All employment decisions, including hiring, are made without regard to protected status. Qualified individuals with disabilities may request reasonable accommodation by contacting the URI benefits office at 401-874-2416. For TTY assistance, contact R.I. Relay Services at 711. Environmental Conditions ENVIRONMENTAL CONDITIONS : This position is not substantially exposed to adverse environmental conditions . Posting Date 07/17/2026 Closing Date Special Instructions to Applicants Please attach the following 2 ( PDF ) documents to your online Employment Application: (#1) Cover letter. (#2) Resume. Note: References will be upon request by the search committee. Quicklink for Posting https://jobs.uri.edu/postings/16847 Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter/Letter of Application Resume Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have a Master's degree in social work, counseling, psychology or college student personnel? Yes No * Do you have a minimum of two years of administrative or instructional experience working with college students in a higher education setting? Yes No * Do you have ability to manage highly sensitive and confidential communications? Yes No * Do you have crisis management experience? Yes No * Do you have experience with issues of disability? Yes No * Do you have working knowledge of Federal Regulations 504, Americans with Disabilities Act Amendment Act, and EERPA? Yes No * Do you have experience with accommodation determinations? Yes No * Do you have strong verbal and interpersonal communication skills? Yes No * Do you have proficiency in written communication skills? Yes No * Do you have understanding of student development theory? Yes No * Do you have knowledge of common issues and challenges facing a diverse population of undergraduate and graduate students? Yes No * Do you have ability to work in a fast—paced, collaborative team environment? Yes No * Do you have willingness to work non-standard hours (e.g. , evenings, weekends, late nights)? Yes No * Do you have experience with technology (i.e. Word, Excel, PowerPoint) : Decision—making skills? Yes No * Do you have ability to work with diverse groups/ populations? Yes No * Are you a URI employee currently in a position covered by the PSA Union? (You will not be disqualified for answering \"No\"). 